---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import { version } from '../../../package.json';
import { examples } from '../../data/examples.mjs';
import html from '../demos/creature.html?raw';
import js from '../demos/creature.js?raw';
const featured = ['temperature-converter', 'dashboard', 'kanban'].map(id => {
  const example = examples.find(example => example.id === id);
  if (!example) throw new Error(`Missing featured example: ${id}`);
  return example;
});
const bootstrap = "\n\ncreateCreature().mount('#creature-builder');";
---
<Site title="Powerfully simple web development">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <a class="eyebrow" href="/releases/"><span class="status-dot"></span> Brio {version} · Small by design. Yours to build with. <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    <h1>Your HTML.<br /><span class="blue">More interactive.</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-description">An HTML-first JavaScript framework.<br />Bring a little life to a page—or build a whole app.<br /><strong>Start small. See where it takes you.</strong></p>
    <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/start/getting-started/">Build your first thing <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button" href="#demo-heading">Play a little ↓</a></div>
    <div class="install"><code>npm install @jnreis/briojs</code><button id="copy-install" type="button" aria-label="Copy npm install command">Copy</button></div>
    <p class="small muted">No virtual DOM. No required compiler. No runtime dependencies.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap demo-section" aria-labelledby="demo-heading">
    <div class="section-intro"><p class="overline">Small code. Big personality.</p><h2 id="demo-heading">Make a little creature.</h2><p>Pick a mood. Choose a color. Give it a name.<br />Or hit Surprise me and meet someone new.</p></div>
    <div class="mixer-workshop">
      <div class="workshop-bar"><span><span class="status-dot"></span> The try-it corner</span><span>No install. No wrong answers.</span></div>
      <div class="mixer-stage">
        <div set:html={html}></div>
        <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to build a creature. The source below and all documentation work without it.</p></noscript>
      </div>
      <div class="workshop-notes"><span><code>b-model</code> connects the controls.</span><span><code>b-bind-style</code> paints and sizes it.</span><span><code>b-if</code> swaps the mood extras.</span><span><code>b-on-click</code> rolls the dice.</span></div>
      <details class="mixer-source"><summary>See how it works. <span>Peek at the HTML + JavaScript behind the creature</span></summary>
        <div class="code-demo">
          <div class="code-panels"><div class="code-label"><span class="dot"></span> index.html</div><Code code={html} lang="html" /></div>
          <div class="code-panels"><div class="code-label"><span class="dot"></span> app.js</div><Code code={js + bootstrap} lang="js" /><p class="source-note">The creature is a few spans and ordinary CSS. No canvas, images, or component compiler. <a href="/reference/first-app/">Start with something smaller →</a></p></div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="real-examples-heading">
    <p class="overline">Start small. Build something real.</p>
    <h2 id="real-examples-heading">Go on. Pull a thread.</h2>
    <p class="muted">A useful little tool. A dashboard full of data. A board for the whole project. Try one, then see how it works.</p>
    <div class="cards">
      {featured.map(example => <article class="card">
        <span class="card-number">{example.level}</span>
        <h3>{example.title}</h3>
        <p>{example.description}</p>
        <div class="card-links">
          <a href={`/demos/${example.id}/index.html`}>Try demo →</a>
          <a href={`https://github.com/jnreis/briojs/tree/main/tests/apps/${example.id}`}>Source ↗</a>
        </div>
      </article>)}
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">The dashboard and Kanban use simulated backends, data, or teammates, not hosted services. Some demos save data in your browser. Please don’t enter sensitive information.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="ways-heading">
    <p class="overline">No grand rewrite required</p><h2 id="ways-heading">Start small. Get carried away.</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card"><span class="card-number">01 / WIDGETS</span><h3>A small island of useful.</h3><p>A menu, a form, a calculator. Mount Brio around just the interactive part. Leave your existing app alone.</p><a href="/reference/three-ways-to-use-brio/#a-widget-in-an-existing-app">Add a widget →</a></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card-number">02 / MULTI-PAGE</span><h3>Keep your server. Add some life.</h3><p>Ordinary links, server-rendered pages, and reactive behavior right where you want it. No takeover required.</p><a href="/reference/three-ways-to-use-brio/#multi-page-site">Enhance a page →</a></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card-number">03 / APPLICATIONS</span><h3>Room to grow.</h3><p>Components, routing, shared state, async resources, and cleanup. The pieces for an app, without the ceremony.</p><a href="/reference/three-ways-to-use-brio/#single-page-app">Build an app →</a></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section features" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
    <div><p class="overline">Simple does not mean limited</p><h2 id="features-heading">Small ideas.<br />Serious capabilities.</h2><a class="text-link" href="/reference/html-directives/">Explore the reference →</a></div>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article><h3>HTML is the template</h3><p>Bindings, models, keyed lists, transitions, and teleports. Familiar markup, a few powerful directives.</p></article>
      <article><h3>State that stays in sync</h3><p>Fine-grained reactivity, computed values, explicit batching, and cancellable async resources.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Cleanup is part of the design</h3><p>Scopes give effects and resources a lifetime. Remove a component, release its behavior.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Only what you need</h3><p>Optional routing, persistence, HTTP, validation, and tools. Tree-shakable ESM with TypeScript declarations.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap closing">
    <p class="overline">Your turn</p><h2>Got a small idea?</h2><p>That’s plenty to start with. Let’s put it on a page.</p><a class="button primary" href="/start/getting-started/">Make your first Brio app →</a>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { createCreature } from '../demos/creature.js';
    const creature = createCreature();
    creature.mount('#creature-builder');
    addEventListener('pagehide', event => { if (!event.persisted) creature.dispose(); });
  </script>
  <script is:inline>
    document.querySelector('#copy-install').addEventListener('click', async event => {
      const button = event.currentTarget;
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText('npm install @jnreis/briojs');
        button.textContent = 'Copied!';
        setTimeout(() => { button.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 2000);
      } catch (error) {
        button.textContent = 'Select command to copy';
        console.error('Unable to copy installation command.', error);
      }
    });
  </script>
</Site>
